E=[
(1,1826,"Sky","Something falls at Saarbruck","A grayish object about three and a half feet long is seen rapidly approaching the earth near Saarbruck. It makes a sound like thunder and expands like a sheet. Fort cites the American Journal of Science.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 1-26-133","Saarbruck, April 1, 1826"),
(2,1889,"Falls","Darkness and ice chunks at Aitkin","Intense darkness falls at Aitkin, Minnesota, and sand and 'solid chunks of ice' are reported to have fallen. Fort cites Science for the account.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Science, April 19, 1889","Intense darkness at Aitkin, Minn., April 2, 1889"),
(4,1846,"Falls","Gelatinous nuggets at Wilna","In a rainstorm at Wilna, Lithuania, nut-sized masses fall that are both resinous and gelatinous. They are odourless until burned, then give off a pronounced sweetish smell.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Comptes Rendus, 23-542","in Wilna, Lithuania, April 4, 1846, in a rainstorm"),
(6,1805,"Falls","Two stones fall at Irkutsk","Two stones fall from the sky at Irkutsk, Siberia, on April 6 (March 25 by the Russian calendar). One is now in the South Kensington Museum. Fort lists Irkutsk with shocks and sounds that recurred there.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1860-12","Upon April 6, 1805, or March 25, according to the Russian calendar"),
(8,1913,"Mysteries","A shadow with no cloud","At Fort Worth, Texas, a shadow appears in the sky, apparently cast by an unseen body. It was supposed to be an unseen cloud, but the patch of shade moved with the declining Sun. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, 41-599","Description of a shadow in the sky, of some unseen body, April 8, 1913, Fort Worth, Texas"),
(9,1876,"Sky","An explosion over Rosenau","A violent explosion is reported over Rosenau, Hungary. Fort pairs it with a luminous object that exploded over Kuttenberg, Bohemia, two years before to the day, and wonders at the repeated local skies.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1877-147","April 9, 1876-an explosion that is said to have been violent, over the town of Rosenau, Hungary"),
(10,1874,"Sky","A terrifying flash over Kuttenberg","A luminous object explodes in the sky of Kuttenberg, Bohemia. The glare is like sunlight, and the 'terrifying flash' is followed by a detonation that rumbles for about a minute.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A.","a luminous object that exploded in the sky of Kuttenberg, Bohemia"),
(11,1860,"Cosmos","An eclipse by something dark","At noon at Pernambuco, with a cloudless sky, the astronomer M. Lias sees the Sun's light suddenly diminish. Darkness increases until Venus is visible. Fort offers it as an eclipse by a vast dark body.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Comptes Rendus, 50-1197","The astronomer is M. Lias: the phenomenon was seen by him, at Pernambuco, April 11, 1860"),
(13,1879,"Cosmos","Harrison's fast object","Henry Harrison of Jersey City, searching for Brorsen's comet, sees an object moving too fast to be a comet. A friend he calls over confirms the sight. Scientific American printed his letter.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 40-294","upon the evening of April 13, 1879, Mr. Harrison was searching for Brorsen's comet"),
(15,1857,"Falls","Kaba's fossil wax","The substance that falls at Kaba, Hungary, is found to contain organic matter 'analogous to fossil waxes'. Fort connects it to the claim that fossils had been found in meteorites.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Phil. Mag., 4-17-425","the substance that fell at Kaba, Hungary, April 15, 1857, contained organic matter"),
(16,1817,"Mysteries","Howling noises over Palermo","Palermo, Italy has howling noises in the air and large spots obscuring the Sun. Fort lists it among earthquake reports that came with strange things in the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","Palermo, Italy, April 16, 1817"),
(17,1886,"Falls","Red, blue and white hail in Venezuela","In a small Venezuelan town hailstones fall, some red, some blue, some whitish, per a plain-spoken informant who had never heard of similar Russian reports. A correspondent to Nature relayed it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 34-123","there, April 17, 1886, had fallen hailstones, some red, some blue, some whitish"),
(18,1849,"Mysteries","An unknown substance on Mt. Ararat","Shepherds near Mt. Ararat find a substance not native to the area, covering an area 8 to 10 miles around. Presumably it fell there. Timb's Year Book reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Timb's Year Book, 1850-241","upon April 18 or 20, 1849, shepherds near Mt. Ararat"),
(19,1897,"Sky","Airship over Sistersville","A luminous object flashing brilliant red, white and green lights approaches Sistersville, West Virginia from the northwest. The New York Herald reported it. Fort places it in the airship wave of April 1897.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Herald, April 20, 1897","at Sistersville, W. Va., a luminous object had approached the town from the northwest"),
(20,1876,"Falls","Iron falls at Wolverhampton","In a heavy rain, a mass of meteoritic iron falls near Wolverhampton. H. S. Maskelyne accepts it as authentic in Nature. Fort uses it to contrast with similar falls the Museum rejected.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, April 26, 1876; Nature, 14-272","That, April 20, 1876, near Wolverhampton, fell a mass of meteoritic iron"),
(22,1884,"Sky","A red sky over Colchester","At the time of England's severest 19th-century earthquake, a red appearance is seen in the sky over Colchester. The next day a quartz stone breaks a greenhouse in Essex.","NL","New Lands, citing G. P. Yeats; Knowledge, 5-336","there was a red appearance in the sky over Colchester"),
(23,1855,"Falls","A hot light at Achterlyre House","A young woman at Achterlyre House, Crieff sees a luminous object falling, picks it up and drops it because it is hot. Prof. Shepard investigated and thought it possibly fell from the sky.","NL","New Lands, on Prof. Shepard's investigation","upon the night of April 23, 1855, a young woman, in the home of Sir William Murray"),
(24,1887,"Falls","Coke falls from the sky in the Orne","During a thunderstorm in the Orne, France, something falls that 'looked exactly like coke'. Fort cites Nature in his series on cinders and charcoal from the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 36-119","looked exactly like coke"),
(25,1892,"Mysteries","Archdeacon Nouri finds Noah's Ark","Archdeacon Nouri climbs Mt. Ararat and reports finding Noah's Ark. Fort cites the English Mechanic, and notes that at the same time Dr. Holden of Lick Observatory was watching something else.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 56-184","upon April 25, 1892, Archdeacon Nouri climbed Mt. Ararat"),
(26,1884,"Mysteries","Darkness and black rain, Preston and Crowle","A correspondent to Nature reports intense darkness at Preston, England, and another reports black rain at Crowle near Worcester. It returned a week later. Another black rain follows near Church Shetton.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 30-6","an intense darkness at Preston, England, April 26, 1884"),
(27,1820,"Cosmos","Steinheibel's unknown body","Steinheibel in Vienna sees an unknown body, one of the dozens of Vulcan-like sightings Haase collected, which were listed in 1872 by Wolf.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Notices, 1862","Observation by Steinheibel, at Vienna, April 27, 1820"),
(28,1888,"Mysteries","The Barisal Guns begin","Reports begin of the Barisal Guns, mysterious booming sounds heard in Bengal. They continue until March 1, 1889. Fort cites the Proceedings of the Asiatic Society of Bengal.","NL","New Lands, citing Proc. Asiatic Soc. of Bengal, 1889-199","heard between April 28, 1888, and March 1, 1889"),
(30,1887,"Falls","Thick black rain at Castlecommon","A 'thick, black rain' falls at Castlecommon, Ireland. Fort lists it among black rains with those at Slains and the Clyde Valley. It was reported in the American Meteorological Journal.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Met. Jour., 4-193","The rain at Castlecommon, Ireland, April 30, 1887"),
]
